# Standard 1 — Living Governance Documents

**Added:** 2026-07-08

Constitutional Governance documents are living governance authorities.

Unlike Architectural Decision Records (ADR), Constitutional Governance documents are expected to evolve as additional governance standards are approved.

Each Constitutional Governance document governs one enterprise subject area and serves as the single authoritative source for that subject.

---

# Standard 2 — Governance Consolidation Principle

**Added:** 2026-07-08

Related constitutional standards shall be consolidated within a single Constitutional Governance document whenever practical.

The objective is to:

- maintain one authoritative source per governance subject
- improve enterprise searchability
- reduce document fragmentation
- simplify long-term maintenance
- improve AI retrieval quality
- improve architectural traceability

New related governance standards should normally be added to the existing Constitutional Governance document rather than creating a new document.

---

# Standard 3 — Naming Convention

**Added:** 2026-07-08

Constitutional Governance document titles intentionally use the plural term **Standards**.

Each document represents a governed collection of related constitutional standards within a single enterprise subject area.

Example:

- CG-EP-001 Enterprise Persistence Ownership Standards
- CG-EP-002 Security Framework Reference Standards
- CG-EP-003 Constitutional Governance Documentation Standards

---

# Standard 4 — Document History

**Added:** 2026-07-08

Every Constitutional Governance document shall contain a **Document History** section immediately following the document metadata.

Document History records the evolution of the document itself.

Required format:

| Date | Change | Author |

Document History shall remain current throughout the life of the document.

---

# Standard 5 — Entry-Level Governance Timestamps

**Added:** 2026-07-08

Every constitutional standard contained within a Constitutional Governance document shall include an entry-level timestamp.

Formats:

New Standard

**Added:** YYYY-MM-DD

Revision

**Revised:** YYYY-MM-DD — Brief description

Exception

**Exception Added:** YYYY-MM-DD — Specific engagement or requirement

No constitutional governance standard shall exist without an associated timestamp.

---

# Standard 6 — Retroactive Entries

**Added:** 2026-07-08

If historical governance entries require reconstruction from repository history, the date shall be identified as estimated.

Example:

**Added:** YYYY-MM-DD *(Date estimated from repository history and applied retroactively.)*

Transparency shall always take precedence over appearance.

---

# Standard 7 — Governance Searchability

**Added:** 2026-07-08

Constitutional Governance documentation shall be organized to optimize:

- enterprise search
- AI retrieval
- semantic discovery
- repository navigation
- future Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG)
- long-term knowledge management

Documentation organization shall prioritize a single authoritative governance source for each enterprise subject.

---

# Standard 8 — Document Classification

**Added:** 2026-07-08

The HCGO documentation library distinguishes document families according to enterprise responsibility.

Current classifications include:

- ARCH — Architectural Specifications
- ADR — Architectural Decision Records
- CG — Constitutional Governance Standards
- DF — Data Framework Specifications
- PA — Platform Architecture Specifications
- IR — Implementation Rules

Each document family serves a distinct constitutional responsibility.

---

# Standard 9 — Governance Authority

**Added:** 2026-07-08

Constitutional Governance documents establish enterprise governance standards.

Architecture, implementation, product development, patents, SBIR submissions, technical documentation, marketing material, and future enterprise artifacts shall conform to applicable Constitutional Governance Standards.

Where conflicts exist, Constitutional Governance Standards take precedence unless superseded by higher constitutional authority.

---

# Standard 10 — Future Expansion

**Added:** 2026-07-08

Constitutional Governance documents are intentionally designed for long-term enterprise evolution.

Additional related standards shall be incorporated into existing Constitutional Governance documents whenever practical.

Creation of additional Constitutional Governance documents should occur only when a new enterprise governance subject area is introduced.

---

# Architectural Rationale

This governance methodology provides:

- Single authoritative governance source
- Reduced governance fragmentation
- Improved auditability
- Improved enterprise maintainability
- Improved AI-assisted development
- Improved repository searchability
- Improved constitutional traceability
- Long-term scalability for HCGO Enterprise Architecture

---

# Governing Principle

Constitutional Governance documents are living enterprise governance authorities.

They shall remain consolidated, traceable, timestamped, searchable, and maintained as the single authoritative source for their respective governance subject area throughout the lifecycle of the HCGO Enterprise Architecture.